// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #10165
// packageRules cannot combine both matchUpdateTypes and rangeStrategy
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"packageRules cannot combine both matchUpdateTypes and rangeStrategy","body":"19 days ago Renovate [opened an issue](https://github.com/NordicSemiconductor/asset-tracker-cloud-code-style-js/issues/81), stating that the configuration is invalid (it used to work however):\r\n\r\n> Error type: The renovate configuration file contains some invalid settings\r\n> Message: `packageRules[0]: packageRules cannot combine both matchUpdateTypes and rangeStrategy. Rule: {\"automerge\":true,\"rangeStrategy\":\"bump\",\"matchUpdateTypes\":[\"minor\",\"pin\",\"digest\"],\"matchDepTypes\":[\"dependencies\",\"devDependencies\",\"optionalDependencies\",\"peerDependencies\"]}, packageRules[1]: packageRules cannot combine both matchUpdateTypes and rangeStrategy. Rule:`\r\n\r\nHowever, after reviewing the documentation, I don't see why I can't and should not longer be able to specify this configuration:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"extends\": [\"config:base\"],\r\n  \"ignorePresets\": [\":prHourlyLimit2\", \":prConcurrentLimit20\"],\r\n  \"packageRules\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"minor\", \"pin\", \"digest\"],\r\n      \"automerge\": true,\r\n      \"rangeStrategy\": \"bump\",\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"patch\", \"lockFileMaintenance\"],\r\n      \"automerge\": true,\r\n      \"automergeType\": \"branch\",\r\n      \"rangeStrategy\": \"bump\",\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```","author":{"login":"coderbyheart"},"createdAt":"2021-05-26T10:50:00Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"`rangeStrategy` is used *before* update types are assigned. Therefore you can't assign a `rangeStrategy` with a rule that matches update types, because it will be assigned too late. Try splitting your rules:\r\n\r\n```\r\n{\r\n  \"extends\": [\"config:base\"],\r\n  \"ignorePresets\": [\":prHourlyLimit2\", \":prConcurrentLimit20\"],\r\n  \"packageRules\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"rangeStrategy\": \"bump\",\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"minor\", \"pin\", \"digest\"],\r\n      \"automerge\": true,\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"rangeStrategy\": \"bump\",\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"matchUpdateTypes\": [\"patch\", \"lockFileMaintenance\"],\r\n      \"automerge\": true,\r\n      \"automergeType\": \"branch\",\r\n      \"depTypeList\": [\r\n        \"dependencies\",\r\n        \"devDependencies\",\r\n        \"optionalDependencies\",\r\n        \"peerDependencies\"\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2021-05-26T10:56:25Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #16407
// Is it possible to set rangeStrategy per matchUpdateTypes
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"Is it possible to set `rangeStrategy` per `matchUpdateTypes`","body":"I was hoping to set the `rangeStragegy` for major updates to be different to minor and patch updates.  I've tried this a few different ways in the config (using [minor](https://docs.renovatebot.com/configuration-options/#minor) & [patch](https://docs.renovatebot.com/configuration-options/#patch) config and also the [packageRules](https://docs.renovatebot.com/configuration-options/#packagerules)).  I noticed in [this issue](https://github.com/renovatebot/renovate/discussions/14369#discussioncomment-2235354) that `matchUpdateTypes` happens after `rangeStrategy` in the `packageRules` and was wondering if this is also the case for the `minor` and `patch` configuration options.\r\n\r\nTLDR: Is it possible to split `rangeStrategy` based on the update type (major, minor, patch)","author":{"login":"mtmeyer"},"createdAt":"2022-07-04T02:55:52Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"No, because range strategy is used to decide major/minor. What are you trying to achieve?","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2022-07-04T04:04:57Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #33609
// Running multiple Renovate instance against the same repository with different configuration
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"Running multiple Renovate instance against the same repository with different configuration","body":"### How are you running Renovate?\r\n\r\nSelf-hosted Renovate\r\n\r\n### If you're self-hosting Renovate, tell us which platform (GitHub, GitLab, etc) and which version of Renovate.\r\n\r\nGithub, Renovate 39.107.0\r\n\r\n### Please tell us more about your question or problem\r\n\r\nI have a repository that contains _massive_ amounts of Terraform, as well as Kubernetes-related things like Helm charts, ArgoCD apps etc.\r\n\r\nRenovate is currently configured to run on this repository and handle helm, argocd, docker, npm, and terraform.\r\n\r\nRenovate config is currently located under `.github/renovate.json5`.\r\n\r\nWith all these managers enabled, Renovate consistently stalls and never finishes executing (i.e., no logs, no CPU activity in the container, runs for days until forcibly terminated).\r\n\r\nRunning Renovate with _only_ the `terraform` manager succeeds, and running Renovate with managers _other_ than `terraform` also succeeds.\r\n\r\nI would like to split this one Renovate into two - one for terraform, one for everything else. My thinking was to create two files - `renovate.json5` and `renovate-terraform.json5` (or whatever filenames) and point each Renovate instance at a single file. However, I cannot figure out how to actually do such a thing. Is this even possible?\r\n\r\n### Logs (if relevant)\r\n\r\n_No response_","author":{"login":"diversario"},"createdAt":"2025-01-14T13:32:25Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"not possible","author":{"login":"viceice"},"createdAt":"2025-01-17T17:54:31Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #9216
// Multiple config files in a single repo
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"Multiple config files in a single repo","body":"I'm not sure if this is a supported use case, but I was wondering if there could be multiple renovate config files in a single repo. My use case is as follows:\r\n\r\n- Monorepo with team-based ownership of subfolders\r\n- Ideally, renovate could be executed for each team with their own configuration such as managers, exclude paths, package ignores etc as well as assignees\r\n\r\nMaybe I'm going about this the wrong way, but the straightforward solution seemed to be to have multiple renovate configs each with a single `includePaths`. This works except that renovate closes pull requests which it thinks should be cleaned up. For example, if config A runs an npm manager and config B runs a gomod manager, then running config B after config A seems to close all npm related pull requests.\r\n\r\nThank you in advance for your help!","author":{"login":"jdechicchis"},"createdAt":"2021-03-18T19:21:16Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"You cannot have multiple config files, but you can control your configuration fully through `packageRules`, each of which can have a `matchPaths` or `matchFiles` field to limit the rule. You can also add a \"description\" field to each rule to state who owns it and why it's there, to avoid confusion.","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2021-03-18T20:13:04Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #24416
// How to use multiple instances used in a monorepo (principle of least privilege)
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"How to use multiple instances used in a monorepo (principle of least privilege)","body":"### How are you running Renovate?\n\nSelf-hosted\n\n### If you're self-hosting Renovate, tell us what version of Renovate you run.\n\n36.74.0\n\n### If you're self-hosting Renovate, select which platform you are using.\n\nGitHub Enterprise Server\n\n### Was this something which used to work for you, and then stopped?\n\nI never saw this working\n\n### Wanted end result.\n\nWe have a monorepo containing infrastructure configuration. In this monorepo we have multiple target environments, and we want to update versions to be deployed into each environment.\r\n\r\nProblem is that for some environments (eg. aws govcloud) we are not allowed to use the same credentials to query versions with datasources (eg. aws-machine-image), because of regulatory reasons (principle of least privilege is required, we cannot have a single AWS principal read release versions across all environments).\r\n\r\nSo  essentially because of principle of least privilege requirements we are not allowed to run a single renovate instance.\r\n\r\nIdeally we'd run multiple renovate instances against the same monorepo, one maintaining dependencies in one part of the repo, and another maintaining dependencies in another part of the repo.\r\n\r\nAs far as I can tell renovate doesn't support this use-case yet, it only supports a single renovate.json.\r\n\r\nAny ideas how to tackle this challenge? \r\nWould renovate team be open to accept a PR where we add support for such behaviour (configure on the server-side a custom location where to look for renovate.json)?\n\n### What you tried so far.\n\nSplitting up the repo, but that goes against our monorepo preference.\n\n### Relevant debug logs\n\n_No response_","author":{"login":"reegnz"},"createdAt":"2023-09-14T07:14:04Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"I'm fairly uninterested in seeing such capability added natively because it likely will cause complexity both for users as well as for maintainers, while being non applicable to 99.9% of users (or perhaps more).\r\n\r\nHowever you are still welcome to _propose_ specifically how this would work and assess how much source code changes would be required, so we can assess if in fact it's elegant enough to not be a future burden.\r\n\r\nYou may be able to achieve what you want today with the following:\r\n- Use a different bot identity (either app or user PAT) for each bot\r\n- Do not use repo config (e.g. `renovate.json`) except if it's common for all bots\r\n- Use different global config (e.g. `config.js`) for each bot\r\n- Use a different `branchPrefix` for each bot so that they can ignore each others' branches","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2023-09-14T07:19:49Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #14727
// Is it safe to run multiple renovate instances on the same host?
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"Is it safe to run multiple renovate instances on the same host?","body":"Hi,\r\nI am running the self-hosted renovate slim container image on a VM. I have Jenkins in place with multiple executors on the same worker node and schedule each repository update individually. I ensure that only a single renovate instance is working on the same repo at the same time via Jenkins locks, but as I use binarySource=docker and mount the docker socket into the renovate container, I am wondering if that is safe to do. I noticed that renovate tries to clean dangling containers, could that overlap between multiple executions?\r\n\r\nAs I run renovate on quite a few repos, I would like Jenkins to run the updates in parallel.\r\n\r\nWould it be possible to disable the cleanup in renovate and instead to manage it separately, e.g. periodic cleanups via Jenkins? \r\n\r\nThanks in advance\r\nKonrad","author":{"login":"konrad-ohms"},"createdAt":"2022-03-18T16:37:10Z","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"Yes, you need to make sure renovate doesn't use same work directory (baseDir) \n\nYou also need to configure this:\n\nhttps://docs.renovatebot.com/self-hosted-configuration/#dockerchildprefix","author":{"login":"viceice"},"createdAt":"2022-03-18T19:20:34Z"}]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate discussion #12927
// Package rules for currently pinned dependencies
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"discussion":{"title":"Package rules for currently pinned dependencies","body":"Is there a way to target currently pinned packages in `packageRules`?\r\n\r\nMy use-case (similar to https://github.com/renovatebot/renovate/issues/10980) is that generally we use caret ranges, but when we pin a package, it's usually because of an issue with more recent versions of that package that need to be resolved, or that we could resolve but can't do so immediately because it's a more involved fix.\r\n\r\nWe also group all non-major updates together. I'd like for pinned versions not to be picked up by that grouping (suggested [here](https://github.com/renovatebot/renovate/issues/3386#issuecomment-473165158) that we could make a package rule with a `null` `groupName`). I could just use `updatePinnedDependencies: false`, but it would be preferable to still get PRs made - just separately from our group.\r\n\r\nFrom what I can tell, `matchUpdateTypes: ['pin']` would not be correct, as I assume this targets updates that specifically change a non-pinned version to a pinned version.\r\n\r\nAs for `matchCurrentVersion`, I assume this only matches against the current \"absolute\" version, and not the version range currently configured.\r\n\r\nI could be interpreting those wrong though, which is why I could use some help finding a solution 😃 ","author":{"login":"joekur"},"createdAt":"2021-12-02T19:02:09Z","comments":{"nodes":[]}}}}}

// ======================================================================
// SOURCE: renovatebot/renovate issue #16547
// Gemfile not updating when rangeStrategy=update-lockfile (current state, resolves prior spike uncertainty)
// ======================================================================
{"data":{"repository":{"issue":{"title":"Gemfile not updating when rangeStrategy=update-lockfile","state":"CLOSED","stateReason":"COMPLETED","closedAt":"2022-12-07T15:00:51Z","body":"### How are you running Renovate?\n\nMend Renovate hosted app on github.com\n\n### If you're self-hosting Renovate, tell us what version of Renovate you run.\n\n_No response_\n\n### Please select which platform you are using if self-hosting.\n\n_No response_\n\n### If you're self-hosting Renovate, tell us what version of the platform you run.\n\n_No response_\n\n### Was this something which used to work for you, and then stopped?\n\nI never saw this working\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nThe docs for rangeStrategy=update-lockfile say that it will fallback to rangeStrategy=replace when the new version is not supported by the existing range. Given flipper@<~ 0.22.2, which wouldn't support 0.25.0, Renovate tries to update the lockfile but then fails to run the lockfile update command since the Gemfile doesn't support the new version. Why isn't it updating the Gemfile?\r\n\r\nFor reproduction, see https://github.com/narwold/renovate-test/blob/main/renovate.json\r\nNote that it does update the Gemfile when using the `replace` strategy (see previous attempt at https://github.com/narwold/renovate-test/pull/6)\r\n\r\nSee previous discussion at https://github.com/renovatebot/renovate/discussions/16369\n\n### Relevant debug logs\n\nThe log specifically mentions this error:\r\n```\r\nBundler could not find compatible versions for gem \\\"flipper\\\":\\n  In snapshot (Gemfile.lock):\\n    flipper (>= 0.25.0)\\n\\n  In Gemfile:\\n    flipper\\n\\n    flipper-redis (~> 0.22.2) was resolved to 0.22.2, which depends on\\n      flipper (~> 0.22.2)\\n\\nRunning `bundle update` will rebuild your snapshot from scratch, using only\\nthe gems in your Gemfile, which may resolve the conflict.\\n\r\n```\r\n...and if you run `bundle install` locally, it's the same result. If you manually update the Gemfile, then it works.\n\n### Have you created a minimal reproduction repository?\n\nI have linked to a minimal reproduction repository in the bug description","comments":{"nodes":[{"body":"Does it only happen if you group the flipper dependencies? And can you duplicate it to a second repo with rangeStrategy=replace to show the working example?","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2022-07-12T15:24:11Z"},{"body":"I believe it was happening before I grouped them, but I'll double-check.","author":{"login":"narwold"},"createdAt":"2022-07-13T15:32:03Z"},{"body":"Always, the more minimal the better ","author":{"login":"rarkins"},"createdAt":"2022-07-13T19:18:41Z"},{"body":"OK, so it does appear to work without the grouping, so it's an issue w/ grouping. The additional annoyance, of course, is that flipper can't bump without flipper-redis, so flipper-redis has to go first, but that doesn't stop Renovate from opening a \"nothing PR\" for flipper at https://github.com/narwold/renovate-test/pull/8. Of course, presumably this would only happen once and wouldn't happen again after the x86_64-linux platform is added to the lockfile.","author":{"login":"narwold"},"createdAt":"2022-07-13T21:20:09Z"}]}}}}}
